The '''Hantek 1008C''' is a USB-based, 8-channel oscilloscope with 12-bit sampling resolution. The hardware also includes an 8-channel digital pattern generator, which is not currently supported by the sigrok driver.

See [[Hantek 1008C/Info]] for some more details (such as '''lsusb -vvv''' output) on the device.

== Driver support ==

The device is supported by the {{LSR}} <code>hantek-1008c</code> driver. It supports:

* All eight analog input channels, including sparse channel selections.
* Trigger, Scan, and Roll acquisition modes.
* Channel-count-dependent sample-rate lists.
* Rising and falling edge triggers on any enabled analog channel.
* Trigger levels expressed in volts and converted using the selected input range and channel calibration.
* Narrow, Medium, and Wide input ranges.
* Optional per-channel and per-range voltage calibration.
* Automatic recovery from the temporary USB disconnects and readiness failures observed during acquisition setup.

The digital pattern-generator outputs are not supported.

=== Acquisition modes ===

; Trigger
: Captures frames from the device's acquisition memory. Hardware triggering is available on any enabled channel, with rising or falling slope and a trigger level expressed in volts. With no trigger source selected, acquisition runs in automatic/free-running mode and forces completion after the trigger wait expires.

; Scan
: Uses the device's official low-rate scan protocol. Samples from all enabled channels are interleaved continuously. Available per-channel rates depend on the number of enabled channels.

; Roll
: Uses the device's rolling acquisition protocol. It provides more low-rate samples than Scan mode, at the cost of one auxiliary word in each interleaved row. Available per-channel rates depend on the number of enabled channels.

The driver reports the effective '''per-channel''' sample rate. The maximum aggregate Trigger rate is 2.4MS/s. Trigger uses physical acquisition widths of 1, 2, 4, 4, 6, 6, 8, and 8 lanes for one through eight enabled channels respectively. Scan divides its aggregate rate by the exact enabled-channel count. Roll divides its aggregate rate by the enabled-channel count plus one.

=== Input ranges ===

The driver presents the hardware A2 settings using user-facing names:

{| class="wikitable"
! Name
! Hardware value
! Nominal scale
|-
| Narrow
| <code>A2=01</code>
| 0.0002V/count
|-
| Medium
| <code>A2=02</code>
| 0.00125V/count
|-
| Wide
| <code>A2=03</code>
| 0.01V/count
|}

These are hardware input-range selections, not volts-per-division settings. The displayed volts-per-division scale is controlled separately by the frontend.

=== Calibration ===

The driver can apply optional zero-offset and voltage-scale calibration independently for every channel and input range. Install libsigrok, close PulseView and any other program using the oscilloscope, then run:

  sigrok-hantek-1008c-calibrate

The utility groups the three grounded captures and then the supported onboard-reference checks for each channel, minimizing probe moves. Follow its connection prompts carefully. During a grounded capture, disconnect or ground every other input. Do not leave the onboard reference output connected to another channel.

Calibration data is stored at:

  $XDG_DATA_HOME/hantek-1008c/calibration.ini

If <code>XDG_DATA_HOME</code> is not set, the default is:

  ~/.local/share/hantek-1008c/calibration.ini

Calibration entries are currently keyed by USB connection path. Moving the oscilloscope to another USB port may require recalibration or moving the applicable entries to sections for the new connection path.

The utility rejects noisy grounded captures and unusually large changes from an existing zero calibration. The maximum permitted change is recorded as a device policy in the calibration file and can be deliberately changed with the utility's <code>--max-zero-calibration-range</code> option.

=== PulseView ===

Recent PulseView versions expose Device mode, Input range, sample rate, trigger source, trigger slope, and trigger level in the device configuration. Changing the device mode or enabled-channel set refreshes the available sample-rate list.

